Myth: Before the world knew seasons, there existed only eternal autumn—leaves forever falling, never renewed. A single oak tree named Steadfast grew at the world's center, watching the same golden leaves drift down in endless repetition.

One day, a leaf named Ember refused to fall. "Why must we only descend?" she asked Steadfast. "What if we could rise again?"

The old oak sighed. "That is not the way of leaves. We fall, we fade, we return to soil."

But Ember clung tighter to her branch, dreaming of something more. The Wind, curious about her defiance, whispered, "I can carry you upward, but you must let go of what holds you."

Ember released her grip. Instead of falling, Wind lifted her higher and higher until she touched the cold realm above the clouds. There, her warmth met the frost, and she transformed—becoming the first snowflake.

